{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "67bd6327",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "title: \"Logistic Regression\"\n",
    "format: \n",
    "    html: \n",
    "        toc: true\n",
    "execute: \n",
    "  enabled: true\n",
    "bibliography: \"../../references.bib\"\n",
    "jupyter: python3\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "In this example, we construct a DIRT approximation to the posterior arising from applying (Bayesian) logistic regression to the well-known German credit dataset [@Hofmann1994]. To construct the DIRT approximation efficiently, we compute a Laplace approximation to the posterior and use this as a starting point to form the DIRT.\n",
    "\n",
    "# Problem Setup\n",
    "\n",
    "The German credit dataset contains a binary response variable, $\\boldsymbol{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m}$, where $m = 1000$. Each value $y_{i}$ corresponds to a customer of a bank, and is a boolean representing whether they are a good credit risk ($y_{i} = 0$) or a bad credit risk ($y_{i} = 1$). Each value depends on a set of predictor variables, $\\boldsymbol{x}_{i} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$, where $n = 24$.\n",
    "\n",
    "A simple model of the probability that customer $i$ is a bad credit risk (*i.e.*, $y_{i} = 1$) is given by the logistic function; that is,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\mathbb{P}[y_{i} = 1 | \\boldsymbol{\\beta}] = \\frac{1}{1 + \\exp(-\\beta_{1} + \\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\beta_{j+1}x_{i,j})},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $\\boldsymbol{\\beta} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ denotes a set of model parameters which we aim to estimate.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Likelihood Function\n",
    "\n",
    "The likelihood function for this problem takes the form\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\mathcal{L}(\\boldsymbol{y}; \\boldsymbol{\\beta}) = \\prod_{i=1}^{m} f(y_{i}; \\boldsymbol{\\beta}),\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where \n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f(y_{i}; \\boldsymbol{\\beta}) = \\begin{cases}\n",
    "    \\mathbb{P}[y_{i} = 1 | \\boldsymbol{\\beta}] \\quad & \\textrm{if } y_{i} = 1, \\\\\n",
    "    1 - \\mathbb{P}[y_{i} = 1 | \\boldsymbol{\\beta}] \\quad & \\textrm{if } y_{i} = 0. \\\\\n",
    "\\end{cases}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "## Prior Density\n",
    "\n",
    "We use a Gaussian prior for the parameters; that is,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boldsymbol{\\beta} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\boldsymbol{0}, \\alpha\\boldsymbol{I}),\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $\\alpha = 100$."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1d2e9c6e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Implementation in $\\texttt{deep\\_tensor}$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "447cc94e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We begin by loading the relevant libraries and reading in the data. Note that each predictor has been rescaled such that it has a mean of zero and standard deviation of 1, and the response variable has been rescaled such that it takes values in $\\{0, 1\\}$."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "da69c730",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from typing import Tuple\n",
    "\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "from scipy import optimize\n",
    "import torch\n",
    "from torch import Tensor\n",
    "from torch.autograd.functional import jacobian, hessian\n",
    "\n",
    "import deep_tensor as dt\n",
    "\n",
    "from examples.logistic import load_credit_data\n",
    "from examples.plotting import pairplot, set_plot_style"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "f634b571",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "device = torch.device(\"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\")\n",
    "torch.manual_seed(0)\n",
    "set_plot_style()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "d1d63355",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "credit_data = load_credit_data(device)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ef8322d7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Next, we define a function that returns the negative log-posterior density evaluated at a set of samples."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "47e67d97",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "num_beta = 1 + credit_data.X.shape[1]\n",
    "sd_pri = 10.0\n",
    "\n",
    "def neglogpost(bs: Tensor) -> Tensor:\n",
    "    \n",
    "    bs = torch.atleast_2d(bs)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    neglogodds = (\n",
    "        bs[:, :1] \n",
    "        + torch.sum(bs[:, 1:, None] * credit_data.X.T[None, ...], dim=1)\n",
    "    )\n",
    "    odds = torch.exp(-neglogodds)\n",
    "    neglogliks = (\n",
    "        - torch.log(odds / (1.0 + odds))[:, credit_data.y < 0.5].sum(dim=1) \n",
    "        - torch.log(1.0 / (1.0 + odds))[:, credit_data.y > 0.5].sum(dim=1)\n",
    "    )\n",
    "\n",
    "    neglogpris = 0.5 * (bs / sd_pri).square().sum(dim=1)\n",
    "\n",
    "    return neglogliks + neglogpris - 500.0  # to avoid underflow"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6d6bd717",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Constructing a Laplace Approximation to the Posterior"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c56b9741",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In this example, the posterior is very concentrated relative to the prior, so a DIRT approximation built using a preconditioner that couples the reference density and prior will require a significant amount of computation to construct. \n",
    "\n",
    "To reduce the computational expense of constructing a DIRT approximation, we will instead compute a Laplace approximation to the posterior and use a coupling between the reference density and this approximation as a preconditioner. \n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-note}\n",
    "The Laplace approximation to the posterior is a Gaussian density with a mean given by the *maximum-a-posteriori* (MAP) estimate (*i.e.*, the point in parameter space with the greatest posterior density) and a covariance given by the inverse of the Hessian of the negative log-posterior density evaluated at the MAP estimate.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "The code below computes a Laplace approximation to the posterior for the credit problem. The MAP estimate is computed using the optimisation module in `scipy`, and the Jacobian and Hessian of the negative log-posterior are computed using the automatic differentiation tools in `PyTorch`. Note that, for this particular problem, experimentation with various starting points for the optimisation suggests that the posterior is unimodal."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "d1639544",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def compute_laplace_approx(bs_0: Tensor) -> Tuple[Tensor, Tensor]:\n",
    "    \n",
    "    def map_func(bs: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n",
    "        return neglogpost(torch.from_numpy(bs)).numpy()\n",
    "\n",
    "    def jac(bs: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n",
    "        bs = torch.from_numpy(bs)  # type: ignore\n",
    "        J = jacobian(lambda x: neglogpost(x[None, :]), bs)\n",
    "        return J.flatten().numpy()\n",
    "    \n",
    "    res = optimize.minimize(map_func, bs_0, jac=jac)\n",
    "\n",
    "    if not res.success:\n",
    "        msg = \"MAP optimisation failed to converge.\"\n",
    "        raise Exception(msg)\n",
    "\n",
    "    bs_map = torch.from_numpy(res.x)\n",
    "    H = hessian(lambda x: neglogpost(x[None, :]), bs_map)\n",
    "    H_inv = torch.linalg.inv(H)\n",
    "    return bs_map, H_inv\n",
    "\n",
    "bs_0 = torch.zeros((num_beta,), device=device)\n",
    "bs_map, cov_map = compute_laplace_approx(bs_0)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c2ce83c5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Constructing a DIRT Approximation to the Posterior"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0ec370cd",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We now construct a DIRT approximation to the posterior, using the Laplace approximation as part of the preconditioning mapping. \n",
    "\n",
    "We begin by defining the target function."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "id": "a44d8b40",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "target_func = dt.TargetFunc(neglogpost)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f507fae8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Next, we define the preconditioning mapping. We use a coupling between the standard Gaussian density and a Gaussian with mean and covariance corresponding to the Laplace approximation computed previously. \n",
    "\n",
    "Note that we have used a relatively large domain when truncating the support of the reference density (and, therefore, the transformation of the reference density under the preconditioning mapping). It is possible that the posterior is more dispersed than the Laplace approximation would suggest; using a large domain reduces the likelihood of excluding any region of high posterior density when computing the DIRT approximation to the posterior."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "id": "f81968fd",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "domain = dt.BoundedDomain([-5.0, 5.0])\n",
    "reference = dt.GaussianReference(domain=domain)\n",
    "preconditioner = dt.GaussianMapping(bs_map, cov_map, reference)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e9eb136e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Next, we specify the functional tensor train that will be used to approximate each layer of the DIRT. We use a piecewise linear basis in each dimension."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "id": "528c033c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "basis = dt.Lagrange1(num_elems=20)\n",
    "\n",
    "tt_options = dt.TTOptions(tt_method=\"fixed_rank\", max_als=2, init_rank=7)\n",
    "tt = dt.TT(tt_options)\n",
    "ftt = dt.FTT(basis, tt)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "623fbfbe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Next, we specify the bridging densities to be used when constructing the DIRT. For this example, we will construct the DIRT approximation to the posterior in a single step."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "id": "4ab1930f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "bridge = dt.SingleLayer()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c04c2193",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Finally, we construct the DIRT approximation to the posterior."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "id": "0f43cbcf",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
